    "['unit [unit_number] family [family_type] [protocol] [metric_value]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'no ipv6 ospf cost 5 interface GigabitEthernet1/0/1', 'parent_command': ['interface [interface_name]'], 'root': 'interface [interface_name]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nno ipv6 ospf cost 5 interface GigabitEthernet1/0/1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. \"delete\" in Juniper translates to \"no\" in Cisco\\n2. \"interfaces ge-1/0/1\" becomes \"interface GigabitEthernet1/0/1\"\\n3. \"family inet6 ospfv3\" is equivalent to IPv6 OSPF in Cisco\\n4. \"metric 5\" translates to \"cost 5\" in Cisco\\'s OSPF terminology\\n5. The \"unit 0\" concept doesn\\'t exist in Cisco, so it\\'s omitted', 'trans_template': 'no ipv6 ospf cost 5 interface GigabitEthernet1/0/1'}]]": 1,
    "['set protocols router-advertisement interface [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ipv6 nd ra suppress all', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'ipv6 nd ra suppress all', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco command for the Juniper `set protocols router-advertisement interface all` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nipv6 nd ra suppress all\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Cisco command is actually the inverse of Juniper's behavior. Juniper's command enables router advertisements on all interfaces, while Cisco by default sends RA on all interfaces and this command suppresses them. To get equivalent behavior to Juniper's configuration, you would actually need to *not* configure this suppression command in Cisco (as RA is enabled by default). \\n\\nHowever, if you specifically need a Cisco command that represents the same intent as the Juniper command (enabling RA on all interfaces), you would use:\\n##\\ninterface range all\\n ipv6 nd ra suppress\\n no ipv6 nd ra suppress\\n##\\n\\nThis explicitly enables RA on all interfaces by removing any potential suppression. But in most cases, no configuration is needed on Cisco as RA is enabled by default.\", 'trans_template': 'ipv6 nd ra suppress all'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'interface range all', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface range all', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco command for the Juniper `set protocols router-advertisement interface all` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nipv6 nd ra suppress all\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Cisco command is actually the inverse of Juniper's behavior. Juniper's command enables router advertisements on all interfaces, while Cisco by default sends RA on all interfaces and this command suppresses them. To get equivalent behavior to Juniper's configuration, you would actually need to *not* configure this suppression command in Cisco (as RA is enabled by default). \\n\\nHowever, if you specifically need a Cisco command that represents the same intent as the Juniper command (enabling RA on all interfaces), you would use:\\n##\\ninterface range all\\n ipv6 nd ra suppress\\n no ipv6 nd ra suppress\\n##\\n\\nThis explicitly enables RA on all interfaces by removing any potential suppression. But in most cases, no configuration is needed on Cisco as RA is enabled by default.\", 'trans_template': 'interface range all'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ipv6 nd ra suppress', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'ipv6 nd ra suppress', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco command for the Juniper `set protocols router-advertisement interface all` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nipv6 nd ra suppress all\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Cisco command is actually the inverse of Juniper's behavior. Juniper's command enables router advertisements on all interfaces, while Cisco by default sends RA on all interfaces and this command suppresses them. To get equivalent behavior to Juniper's configuration, you would actually need to *not* configure this suppression command in Cisco (as RA is enabled by default). \\n\\nHowever, if you specifically need a Cisco command that represents the same intent as the Juniper command (enabling RA on all interfaces), you would use:\\n##\\ninterface range all\\n ipv6 nd ra suppress\\n no ipv6 nd ra suppress\\n##\\n\\nThis explicitly enables RA on all interfaces by removing any potential suppression. But in most cases, no configuration is needed on Cisco as RA is enabled by default.\", 'trans_template': 'ipv6 nd ra suppress'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'no ipv6 nd ra suppress', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'no ipv6 nd ra suppress', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco command for the Juniper `set protocols router-advertisement interface all` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nipv6 nd ra suppress all\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Cisco command is actually the inverse of Juniper's behavior. Juniper's command enables router advertisements on all interfaces, while Cisco by default sends RA on all interfaces and this command suppresses them. To get equivalent behavior to Juniper's configuration, you would actually need to *not* configure this suppression command in Cisco (as RA is enabled by default). \\n\\nHowever, if you specifically need a Cisco command that represents the same intent as the Juniper command (enabling RA on all interfaces), you would use:\\n##\\ninterface range all\\n ipv6 nd ra suppress\\n no ipv6 nd ra suppress\\n##\\n\\nThis explicitly enables RA on all interfaces by removing any potential suppression. But in most cases, no configuration is needed on Cisco as RA is enabled by default.\", 'trans_template': 'no ipv6 nd ra suppress'}]]": 1,
